% PostgreSQL. The third target and the closest match to SEL -- which shows up as
% caveats it does NOT need, and as the one thing it needs that nothing else did:
% every operand's type stated, because it will not coerce.

### name: pg.num.operands-are-cast
--- note
SEL numbers ARE text values (spec §4), so a TEXT-kind fragment that reads as a
number is a legal arithmetic operand -- `x * BACKWARDS(y)` is arithmetic in SEL.
PostgreSQL answers `operator does not exist: numeric * text`. The cast also puts
the two on the same footing: a TEXT operand that does NOT read as a number
raises here, exactly as SEL's E_NOT_NUM does, where SQLite would quietly make it
zero.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
2.50 + 2.50
--- expect
(CAST(2.50 AS NUMERIC) + CAST(2.50 AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: pg.num.division-is-numeric
--- note
/ between two integers TRUNCATES in PostgreSQL -- 10 / 4 is 2 -- which is a
different operation rather than an imprecise one. docs/SQL-TRANSLATION.md §11
predicted this from the manual at M1 and it is now measured. What is left after
the cast is scale: 2.5000000000000000 against SEL's ten digits.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
10 / 4
--- expect
(CAST(10 AS NUMERIC) / CAST(4 AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: pg.num.modulo-is-exact
--- note
No modulo-integer caveat, unlike SQLite: MOD on numeric gives 1.5 for 5.5 % 2.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
5.5 % 2
--- expect
MOD(CAST(5.5 AS NUMERIC), CAST(2 AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: pg.num.round-needs-no-caveat
--- note
round() is half away from zero here -- round(2.5,0) is 3 and round(-2.5,0) is
-3 -- which is SEL's rule, so mysql-family's rounding-mode caveat is absent.
The second argument is cast because round(numeric, int) is the exact overload.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
ROUND(2.5, 0)
--- expect
round(CAST(2.5 AS NUMERIC), CAST(0 AS INTEGER))
===
### name: pg.num.integer-overloads-are-pinned
--- note
The subtle one. Given an INTEGER argument PostgreSQL resolves sign(), power(),
trunc(), ceil() and floor() to their DOUBLE PRECISION overloads --
pg_typeof(sign(13)) is `double precision` -- so an expression that looks exact
quietly leaves exact-decimal territory. Not a type error anybody would have
seen; a precision loss, found by asking the server.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
SIGN(13)
--- expect
sign(CAST(13 AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: pg.text.operands-are-cast
--- note
upper(integer), reverse(numeric) and btrim(numeric, unknown) are all `function
does not exist` here, where MySQL and SQLite coerce without comment.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
UPPER(13 + 4)
--- expect
upper(CAST((CAST(13 AS NUMERIC) + CAST(4 AS NUMERIC)) AS TEXT))
===
### name: pg.text.backwards-is-supported
--- note
reverse() exists here and not in SQLite, which refuses BACKWARDS.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
BACKWARDS("abc")
--- expect
reverse(CAST('abc' AS TEXT))
===
### name: pg.text.collation-is-for-ordering
--- note
COLLATE "C" is needed for the ORDERING half of the byte family, not the equality
half: 'A' = 'a' is already false under the default collation, but 'a' < 'B' is
TRUE there and FALSE under "C". SEL compares bytes, and 'a' is 0x61 against
'B' at 0x42.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
"a" $< "B"
--- expect
(CAST('a' AS TEXT) COLLATE "C" < CAST('B' AS TEXT) COLLATE "C")
===
### name: pg.bin.text-goes-to-bytes-through-convert-to
--- note
This case used to be called `cast-is-idempotent` and expected four nested casts,
with a note arguing that text::bytea::text::bytea recovers the original bytes.
That is true for 'abc' and for every string anyone had tried, and false for any
string containing a backslash: PostgreSQL's text::bytea does not reinterpret
characters as bytes, it parses the text as a bytea INPUT LITERAL, where \\ is
one backslash and \x41 is a byte.

    BLEN('\\')            SEL 2         pg 1
    ENCODE_BASE64('\\')   SEL "XFw="    pg "XA=="
    TO_HEX('a\b')         SEL 615c62    pg 22P02 invalid input syntax for bytea

A sweep of 13,840 renderings of hostile short strings gave 1290 server errors
and 52 silent wrong answers, all on this dialect and none on the other three.
convert_to(text, 'UTF8') is the function that means what SEL means.

The cast is now applied only to a non-BIN operand -- see Emit::fill -- because
bytea->bytea needs nothing and convert_to would not typecheck.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
TO_HEX(TO_UTF8("a"))
--- expect
encode(convert_to(CAST('a' AS TEXT), 'UTF8'), 'hex')
===
### name: pg.refuse.find-with-a-start-position
--- note
strpos takes no start position. This is the case sql/MAP.md §4.1 has cited as
the reason `arity` exists since M1 -- "how PostgreSQL refuses the
three-argument form its POSITION cannot express". Nothing enforced the field
until SQLite exposed it; without that, this entry would have silently dropped
the start position and answered the wrong number.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
FIND("a", "banana", 3)
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: pg.refuse.no-crc32
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
CRC32("x")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: pg.params.question-mark-by-default
--- note
`?` is what a driver wants: PDO_PGSQL rewrites it to the wire protocol's $n, and
a raw $1 handed to PDO is not recognised as a parameter at all -- verified, it
binds nothing and returns NULL.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- note
`&` is the one operand position that is NOT cast: PostgreSQL has
`anynonarray || text`, so 2.50 || 'x' already works and a cast would only add
noise.
--- source
T & "a" & "b"
--- expect
(CAST((CAST("t" AS TEXT) || CAST(? AS TEXT)) AS TEXT) || CAST(? AS TEXT))
--- params
t"a", t"b"
===
### name: pg.params.numbered-for-a-libpq-host
--- note
PostgreSQL's own numbered spelling, for a host that talks libpq directly rather
than through a rewriting driver. Registered at run time, which is what runtime
registration is for -- and this is the first thing in the project to exercise
Emit::placeholder's {n} branch, which has existed since M1 with no user.
--- dialect
pg-numbered
--- mode
params
--- register
[{"dialect": "pg-numbered", "extends": "postgresql", "version": "15", "target": true,
  "lexical": {"placeholder": "${n}"}}]
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T & "a" & "b"
--- expect
(CAST((CAST("t" AS TEXT) || CAST($1 AS TEXT)) AS TEXT) || CAST($2 AS TEXT))
--- params
t"a", t"b"
===
### name: pg.bin.a-bin-operand-is-not-converted-again
--- note
binaryCast converts a TEXT or NUM operand to bytes. An operand that is already
BIN needs no conversion, and here converting it would not even typecheck --
convert_to takes text. Emit::fill skips the cast on a BIN argument, which is
the one place a cast's input kind decides whether it means anything.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
BLEN(TO_UTF8("a"))
--- expect
octet_length(convert_to(CAST('a' AS TEXT), 'UTF8'))
===
